---
/**
 * The long-form version of this argument lives at /learn/relay-with-governance.
 * On the way in, the claim is enough; the reader who wants the mechanism can
 * follow it. Keeping the full essay here made it a third of the landing page.
 */
---

<section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 pb-32">
    <a
        href="/learn/relay-with-governance"
        class="group block rounded-2xl bg-zinc-900/30 border border-zinc-800/50 hover:border-emerald-500/20 hover:bg-zinc-900/50 p-8 md:p-10 transition-all duration-300"
    >
        <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row md:items-center gap-6 md:gap-10">
            <div class="flex-1">
                <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-500 uppercase tracking-wider">
                    Governed async tasks
                </span>
                <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold text-zinc-100 mt-3 mb-3 group-hover:text-emerald-300 transition-colors">
                    Your longest-running operations were the least governed ones
                </h2>
                <p class="text-zinc-400 max-w-2xl">
                    The export, the migration, the bulk write — everything expensive and hard
                    to undo hands back a handle in milliseconds and does the real work later.
                    Hangar governs the relay of that task and the moment it pauses for input,
                    without ever becoming the executor: no scheduler, no job runner, no result
                    store of its own.
                </p>
            </div>
            <span
                class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-emerald-400 group-hover:translate-x-0.5 transition-transform shrink-0"
            >
                Read how the relay works
                <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
            </span>
        </div>
    </a>
</section>
